gns. After the campaign the militia was demobilized. <br>1. Emperor<br>"After AD 89 the title of Ta Chiang-chun or \'Commander-in-Chief\' was a political appointment which carried the responsibilities of a regent."<br>2. ying (division under a chiang-chun, or general) "Records from the north-western garrison give an outline of unit organization at lower levels... Above the hou kuan were the sector headquarters or tu-wei fu for garrison troops, and the division or ying, under a chiang-chun or general, the highest permanent position." <br>Generals could lead campaigns on their own without the presence of the Emperor. e.g. 121-119 BCE campaigns which overthrew "five sub-ordinate Hsiung-nu kingdoms" <br>"A field command was usually an ad hoc appointment for a specific purpose, often reflected in the title given to the recipient - such as \'General Charged With Crossing the Liao\' for a campaign in Korea."<br>3. pu (regiment under a hsiao-wei, or colonel)"... often translated as \'colonel\', was a lower rank used for temporary appointments<br>3. tu-wei fu (sector headquarters)"Records from the north-western garrison give an outline of unit organization at lower levels... Above the hou kuan were the sector headquarters or tu-wei fu for garrison troops, and the division or ying, under a chiang-chun or general, the highest permanent position." <br>4. hou kuan or ch\'u(company under a captain, chun-hou)"Records from the north-western garrison give an outline of unit organization at lower levels: a hou kuan or company usually consisted of five hou (platoons), each with several sui or sections of an officer and four to ten men." <br>5. hou or t\'un(platoon under a commander, t\'un-chang)"Records from the north-western garrison give an outline of unit organization at lower levels: a hou kuan or company usually consisted of five hou (platoons), each with several sui or sections of an officer and four to ten men." <br>6. sui (section, lead by an officer)"Records from the north-western garrison give an outline of unit organization at lower levels: a hou kuan or company usually consisted of five hou (platoons), each with several sui or sections of an officer and four to ten men." <br>7. Individual soldier"Conscripts served mainly as infantry; cavalry was provided by volunteers from noble families or by non-Chinese auxiliaries." \n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1692, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1557})
