   Variable name: military level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Military levels records the Military levels of a polity. Same principle as with Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: military level\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  levels.<br>Military units, like civilian administration, organized with decimal system. <br>1. Sapa Inca<br>King was commander in chief and occasional field general.  Alan Covey: Most military commanders were sons or brothers of the ruler. <br>(2. aucacunakapu (chief of soldiers)came from Hanan Cuzco <br>2. aucata yachachik aup (chief in charge of organizing soldiers)came from Hurin Cuzco <br>2. hinantin aucata suyuchak apu (chief who assigns troops to their proper place)Equivalent to European sergeant major of period <br>2. apusquipay (commander in the field)Usually a relative of the Sapa Inca <br>the apusquiprantin was an assistant of the apusquipay )<br>Alan Covey: Concerning these ranks from aucacunakapu to apusquipay) This all comes from a really spurious late 18th century source (Juan de Velasco), and is not corroborated by any early chronicler  AD: number of ranks corrected to 6 (see hierarchy below).<br>3. hunu kuraka10,000 men were lead by a hunu kuraka - not known how often 10,000 men were fielded <br>4. Waranqa kuraka"One thousand men were commanded by a waranqa kuraka" <br>5. PihcachuncacamayocsPosition held by local leaders <br>The second lowest order was 100 soldiers, under a pachaka kuraka. <br>Alan Covey: Pichka chunka is 50. Pachaka is 100. <br>6. Chuncacamayocs (50 soldiers) Position held by local leaders <br>"The smallest unit contained ten heads of household (hatun runa), under the command of a chunka kamayuq." <br>7. Individual soldier<br><br>aucapussak<br>Huaminca: veteran units from Hanan Cuzco and Hurin Cuzco <br>had captains called aucapussak   Alan Covey: Again, I wouldn’t use information from this source. <br>"Each division contained two halves, each with its own leader." \n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1619, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1481})
