reme commander, the general the top field officer."<br>2. ShakkanakkumGeneral - shakkanakkum.<br>3. General in charge of smaller army divisions (<i>shagina</i>) -- <i>not mentioned by Foster (2016)</i>&gt; Ensi governor  -- <i>not mentioned by Foster (2016); note below</i>&gt; Captain of local detachment -- <i>when not in the field are governors part of the chain of command?</i>&gt; Individual soldier<br>4. Nu-bandanu-banda. "The next command in rank was the nu-banda." Battalion, probably had 600 men. Colonel or major - nubanda.<br>5. UgalaUgala lead smaller unit of about 60. Captain - ugala.<br>6. Individual soldier<br>At Mugdan was "the seat of a manor that dominated the local countryside. ... This manor belong to members of the royal faily or royal household ... managed by a governor (ensi). Other local officials included the shaperum (majordomi) and a captain in command of a detachment of soldiers stationed there."<br>Records from Umma<br>"booty officer(?), the son of a governor in Syria (in training?), equerry, royal commissioner, door attendant, recruiter, courier, cupbearer, minister, quartermaster(?), saperum wuth his clerk, quartermaster of garments(?), physician, porters, constable, an officer in charge of putting identification marks on (captured?) goods (? saper ZAG.SUS), an ass herder, and an assortment of foreigners."\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1498, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1353})
